<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01956</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8616e899-dc3b-4759-a0f0-7dbafeae01c3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
        <exercise id="d21eb73e-0532-407c-8c17-041efa2bbf99" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">

            <para id="para-00002">Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00003">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00004"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00005"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00006"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00007"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00008">Correct! The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6e27480e-e352-49be-9b6f-44f63467d9a5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">

            <para id="para-00011">Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00013"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00014"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00015"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00016"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00017">Correct! The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00018">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00019">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bfabbcb1-2b5d-4d2e-8a10-460822d6bb93" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">

            <para id="para-00020">Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00022"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00023"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00024"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00025"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00026">Correct! The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ba4aa0c4-e1a5-4e68-874d-f6737f811d7f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">

            <para id="para-00029">Choose the quadratic equation that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00030">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00031"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00032"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00033"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00034"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00035">Correct! The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00036">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00037">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_94861d4e-d17e-4df1-9d09-0e8774e8e2c6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00038">factored form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> 
        standard form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00039">The following two equations are equivalent.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">Which equation helps find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts most efficiently?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00041">Correct! The equation written in factored form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> because the factors in the expression allow us to see when the expression is 0.</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00043">The correct answer is factored form. The equation written in factored form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> because the factors in the expression allow us to see when the expression is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e021a697-88ee-4d81-a205-df68f64b7376" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00044">Which equation helps find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept most efficiently?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00045">Correct! The equation written in standard form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the constant term shows us the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
            <para id="para-00046">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00047">The correct answer is standard form. The equation written in standard form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the constant term shows us the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_549853a5-000e-484f-90c1-79f2bae27211" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00048"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> (black graph, at the top, opening upward)
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00049">There are three equations represented on this graph:</para>
            <para id="para-00050">Use the graph to answer questions a and b.</para>
            <para id="para-00051">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_13_01.png"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00052">Which graph represents the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00053">Correct! The blue graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00054">Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00055">The correct answer is blue graph (in the middle, opening upward). The blue graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_10205e3d-e739-41b1-8b9e-175685ffe51b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00056">Which graph represents the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00057">Correct! The green graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative 1, so the graph should open downward.</para>
            <para id="para-00058">Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00059">The correct answer is green graph (at the bottom, opening downward). The green graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the coefficient of the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is a negative 1, so the graph should open downward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4c5dd1eb-ea08-432e-bf96-40ef5667ac83" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00060">Select two equations whose graphs have a positive <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00061">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00062">Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00063">The correct answers are: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5b8cb81c-00b1-4688-b76b-3c1b4ab07a79" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00064">Which function represents the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> shifted 5 units to the right?</para>
            <para id="para-00065">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00066"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00067"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00068"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00069"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00070">Correct! The function is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> because subtracting 5 from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> shifts the graph to the right 5 units.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Subtracting 5 from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> shifts the graph to the right 5 units.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0abd8816-8058-492b-ba67-a32d84901a2c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00073"><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> equations whose graphs open downward.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00074">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00075">Try again. Revisit activity 7.12.2 and activity 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00076">The correct answers are: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_40de6698-d5bb-4a0c-9372-f40f8f6b0a44" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00077">Which curve is the narrowest?</para>
            <para id="para-00078">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00079"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00080"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00081"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00082"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00083">Correct! The function is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is the narrowest because multiplying x by a value greater than 1 makes the curve grow faster. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> , so <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is the narrowest curve.</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00085">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Multiplying <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> by a value greater than 1 makes the curve narrower. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> , so <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is the narrowest curve.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_59bc177a-332c-4bc1-b521-4f21086e378d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00013">

            <para id="para-00086">Which equation is represented by the graph?</para>
            <para id="para-00087">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00088"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00089"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00090"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00091"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00092">Correct! The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the graph is moved 4 units right and 4 units up and the squared term is multiplied by negative 1, so the graph should open downward.</para>
            <para id="para-00093">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00094">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the graph is moved 4 units right and 4 units up and the squared term is multiplied by negative 1, so the graph should open downward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_46ffb136-295e-46ce-9789-3497e2b34928" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00014">

            <para id="para-00095">Which equation is represented by the graph?</para>
            <para id="para-00096">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00097"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00098"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00099"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00100"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            <para id="para-00101">Correct! The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the graph is moved 2 units left and 3 units up and the squared term is multiplied by positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
            <para id="para-00102">Try again. Revisit activities 7.12.2 – 7.12.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00103">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The graph represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> because the graph is moved 2 units left and 3 units up and the squared term is multiplied by positive 1, so the graph should open upward.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1ce92a00-0789-432e-9eee-cadf08545987" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00104">For questions 13 - 14, use the following graph.</para>
            <para id="para-00105">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_14_13_02.png"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00106">Which of the graphs has been horizontally stretched?</para>
            <para id="para-00107">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00108">Graph b</para>
            <para id="para-00109">Graph a</para>
            <para id="para-00110">Graph c</para>
            <para id="para-00111">Graph d</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00112">Correct! Graph d</para>
            <para id="para-00113">Try again. Try Again. Revisit activity 7.12.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00114">The correct answer is Graph d</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0a7c4d1b-b296-460c-b1de-e2e27b90f8d1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00016">
            <para id="para-00115">Which function most closely represents the equation for graph c?</para>
            <para id="para-00116">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00117"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00118"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00119"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00120"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00016">
            <para id="para-00121">Correct! <emphasis/></para>
            <para id="para-00122">Try again. Try Again. Revisit activity 7.12.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00123">The correct answer is <emphasis/></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
